WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.600
So welcome to the
last week of W23.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.060
So from our usual roadmap,
for the last few weeks,

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.820
we have been in the top right
quadrant of our roadmap,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.960
specifically looking at
learning-based methods

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.320
for optimal control.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.182
And over the last
couple of weeks,

00:00:23.182 --> 00:00:25.640
we've basically been introducing
the reinforcement learning

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.840
setting, where we are
looking at ways to estimate

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.417
value functions.

00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.000
Specifically, we introduce Monte
Carlo and temporal difference

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.400
learning as really fundamental,
model-free learning strategies.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.120
And last week, we also looked at
one of the families of methods

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.800
for model-free reinforcement
learning, namely

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.480
value-based methods.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.040
On the other hand,
today, we switch topics

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.600
and we complete, if
you wish, and start

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.400
to discuss the second main
family of methods that

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.780
belong to the model-free
reinforcement learning family,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.460
namely policy optimization.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.320
And as we will see today,
in policy optimization,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.640
differently from what we
did in value-based methods,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.940
whereby the policy is implicitly
defined through the value

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.060
function itself.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.620
In policy
optimization, we'll see

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.500
how we will be representing
the policy explicitly, namely

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.980
through parametric
representation of the policy,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.540
and then tune or
adjust the parameters

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.460
of this parametric
representation of the policy

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.700
to directly maximize the
reinforcement learning

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.460
objective.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.420
But long story
short, today, we'll

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.740
be looking at policy
optimization methods.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.740
Specifically, we'll start
by introducing and defining

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.020
policy gradients,
which will lead us

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.380
into the very first
algorithm, which will

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.720
be the reinforced algorithm.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.980
And we'll do some
discussions on the role

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.925
of variants of these
policy gradient estimators.

00:01:48.925 --> 00:01:51.300
Then we'll be looking at that
actor critic methods, which

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.820
are extremely popular
instantiation of policy

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.310
optimization, and then conclude
with some mention to kind

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.170
of recent reinforcement learning
algorithms and applications.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.510
So let's start
from-- if you recall,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.270
at the beginning
of our discussions

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:13.030
on reinforcement learning, we
loosely introduced reinforcement

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.630
learning as a problem,
where we have an agent that

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.950
interacts with the environment.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.150
Through this
interaction, we generate

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.750
some trajectories style,
which are nothing else

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.870
than a kind of sequences
of states and controls

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.230
that we observe over time.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:33.510
And through this, we defined
the formalism for reinforcement

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.950
learning as a Markov
decision process,

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.030
whereby we defined a
trajectory distribution

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:43.710
p of tau, where p of
tau essentially encodes

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.790
all the stochasticity that
we have in the environment.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.070
Namely, we could
have an initial state

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:54.430
x0, which is sampled from a
distribution over x states.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.690
Concretely, this could be like
the dot reset method in our gym

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.010
environment, for example.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:03.690
We have uncertainty that
comes from the policy itself,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.850
so we are defining a policy
pi as a distribution over

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:10.450
controls given states, and
so this is in itself is also

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.090
a random process.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.370
And then we obviously
discussed also

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.770
the stochasticity that comes
from the transition function

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:21.290
itself, which is a
distribution over next state xt

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:27.110
plus 1 given the current
state xt and the control ut.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:31.610
And so by taking all of these
sources of stochasticity

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.910
and factorizing them, as we've
shown here on this slide,

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:38.170
we basically derive the
probability distribution

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.450
over all possible
trajectories style

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.270
as this factorized
distribution over here.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:48.510
Why was this important?

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.210
It was important
because this enabled

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:53.500
us to define the reinforcement
learning objective, essentially

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:57.460
as a maximization problem,
where we want to basically pick

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.700
the policy pi that maximizes
a certain objective.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:05.540
And this objective is the sum of
discounted cumulative rewards,

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.660
but the sum of discounted
cumulative returns

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.140
is an expectation,
where the expectation is

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.080
under this trajectory
distribution,

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.760
so this is basically where we
started a couple of weeks back.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.720
How does policy optimization
go for solving this problem?

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.740
As we were briefly
mentioning before,

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.660
in policy optimization,
what we do

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.500
is we essentially
define our policy

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.980
to be a parametric
policy, pi of theta,

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:32.900
with some parameters theta.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.900
And therefore, the
reinforcement learning objective

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.980
and the trajectory
distribution itself

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.860
can essentially be
written with respect

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.400
to these parameters theta.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.580
So the initial state
distribution and the transition

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.240
distribution do not
depend on theta directly,

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.520
but our policy does indeed
inform the evolution

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.720
of the trajectory.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.300
And therefore, what we
want to pick essentially,

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.040
is the values of
the parameters theta

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.060
that maximize the RL objective.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.580
So long story short, at face
value in a policy optimization,

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.200
we take the reinforcement
learning objective as it is,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.320
and we basically define
it as a optimization

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:22.520
problem with respect to the
parameters of our policy.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.740
Again, going forward, to
simplify the notation,

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.620
we will assume that this is
an undiscounted environment,

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.640
but everything still
holds when we actually

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.180
account for discounting.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.700
So let us start solving
this problem in practice.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.683
So we saw how--

00:05:44.683 --> 00:05:46.600
well, in value-based
methods, if you remember,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.750
we were ideally solving
this exact same problem,

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.690
but we were doing this
in an indirect way

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.370
because we were doing
essentially fixed point

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:59.170
iteration on q values
and value functions.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:01.570
And then we saw that
that convergence--

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:04.090
we derived some theory,
or, at least, mentioned

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:08.530
some theory for which the
algorithms that we proposed

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.990
would converge to the
optimal value function

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:12.890
and therefore to
the optimal policy.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:14.610
In reinforcement
learning, now we

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:19.890
want to solve this explicit
optimization problem.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.610
So we know that,
before in this class,

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:28.010
we mentioned as a good idea when
we want to solve optimization

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:31.370
problems, that gradient
ascent or gradient descent

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.890
is a good approach for
solving a general class

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:36.770
of numerical
optimization problems.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.370
Fundamentally, if we were
to approach this problem

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.810
through gradient
ascent because we

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.510
want to maximize this
objective function,

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.130
we would need to
first of all estimate

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:49.230
the gradient of the objective
we're interested in,

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:52.230
and then basically cast
learning or learning

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.990
the policy as doing gradient
descent on the objective

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:57.010
that we care about.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:07:00.970
That is, iteratively takes
steps in parameter space,

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:04.810
following the direction that is
given to us by this gradient,

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:09.130
so pictorially, hill
climbing are RL objective.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:16.110
A first question
here is already we

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:20.550
see how our objective
that we wish to optimize

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.510
is a non-trivial
object because we

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:26.030
have this expectation under the
trajectory distribution, which

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:28.310
is something which we
don't really know about

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.190
because the trajectory
distribution, as we said before,

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:34.010
has these three sources
of stochasticity,

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:37.310
so we can assume that
we know our policy

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:41.030
and we can evaluate the
distribution under our policy,

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:43.730
but we don't really know
the transition dynamics,

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:47.810
for example, because
this is the whole point.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:50.570
So our first question
is, how can we

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:53.690
even evaluate this
expectation in the objective?

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.570
And the answer here
is, by basically

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:59.370
leveraging the same tools that
we discussed over and over

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.150
the last couple of weeks,
which is essentially, sampling.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.930
So in reinforcement
learning, whenever

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.610
we don't know some quantity,
for example, the transition

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:12.130
functions, we go around it by
sampling in the environment.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:13.490
What did we mean by sampling?

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.370
So far, is essentially
taking rollouts

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:19.050
and interacting with our
environments over time.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:22.370
So in other words, we
have this objective

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.250
that we wish to
optimize, which entails

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:29.890
computing the expectation under
the trajectory distribution.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:32.929
We don't know how to
compute this expectation,

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:36.289
and so we can approximate
it by running our policy n

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:39.350
times in the environment and
just taking the empirical mean.

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:44.900
So in practice, you
could imagine indeed

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.100
following n possible
rollouts of our policy.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.680
These rollouts will
have some rewards.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.800
We will be observing different
quality of these rollouts,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.300
and then we simply average
out all of the rewards

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:57.600
that we observe over time.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.100
And this would be a
valid approximation

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.560
of this expectation.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:11.220
So we mentioned that we
want to approach this

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.443
through stochastic gradient
ascent or gradient ascent.

00:09:15.443 --> 00:09:16.860
The first thing
that we need to do

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.280
is compute the gradient right.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.380
So what we'll do right now is
basically derive the policy

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:29.140
gradient and specifically derive
actionable kind of expression

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:31.100
of the policy
gradient, and then see

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.820
how we can use this expression
within working algorithms.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.240
Any questions so far?

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:44.240
Great.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:50.560
So for compactness,
let us define

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.600
r of the trajectory tau
as the sum of rewards

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.860
that we observed in a
specific trajectory,

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:00.920
so simply the sum of all the
rewards observed from time 1

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.340
until time t within
that trajectory tau.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.960
So we can use this r tau
to basically rewrite the RL

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:12.980
objective that we were
introducing before,

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:15.600
so J of theta,
which we introduced

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.320
before as being the expectation
under the trajectory

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.180
distribution of
the sum of rewards,

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:23.760
we can just write it
as a small r of tau.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.540
And by definition
of expectation,

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:30.800
we can write this expectation
as the integral of p tau times

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.500
r tau into tau.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.160
So what we said is
what we want to take

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.970
the gradient of this
objective function

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:41.530
and use this to hill climb.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:44.730
So given this
definition or rewriting

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.250
of the objective J of
theta, the gradient

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:50.650
of J of theta with
respect to theta

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:53.690
is the integral of the
gradient with respect

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:58.530
to theta of p tau times r tau
and the tau, so nothing special

00:10:58.530 --> 00:10:59.330
here.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:03.050
We rewrote our RL
objective and then

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:06.550
we're taking the gradient at
face value of that objective.

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:14.010
So here, of course, in--

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.130
this would not be
actionable because we

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.330
would like to
evaluate this gradient

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.890
and basically, take a step in
the direction of that gradient.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:25.850
But the problem here is that
how we define it right now,

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:29.330
basically the gradient
depends on unknown dynamics

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:31.610
and the initial
state distribution

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:37.430
through the probability
of trajectories p tau,

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:39.430
so this is still the
object that we don't really

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:41.450
know how to compute.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.890
So here, essentially, what
we'll be doing in this slide

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:50.470
and the next is that
we'll be basically

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:53.630
massaging this definition
of the policy gradient

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:58.550
until we derive an analytically
tractable and actually

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:02.550
computable expression
of that same gradient.

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:04.210
So the first thing
that we do is,

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:08.430
so we realize that this is the
object that is creating problems

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:11.630
for us, and so we
leverage that identity

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:13.250
on the right of this slide.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:18.230
Specifically, the identity
whereby the probability, p tau,

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:23.710
times the grad log p can
be rewritten as the grad p,

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:24.630
essentially.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:29.190
So we know that the grad of
the logarithm of a function

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:32.910
can be essentially written as
the gradient of that function,

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:35.080
divided by the function itself.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.480
And so the two terms
cancel out, and so we

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:41.960
get that kind of useful
identity on the right.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.800
So specifically, now we
can do this substitution

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.520
in our expression of
the policy gradient.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:53.240
And what we get is that we get
the integral of the p tau, times

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:56.600
the grad log p's, times r tau.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:58.800
Always in the tau, of course.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.480
Now, again, we can use the
definition of expectation.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:09.640
And we can basically rewrite
this integral as the expectation

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:12.000
under the trajectory
distribution

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:13.820
of this part over here.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:22.800
Now, this is not actionable
yet because still we

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:24.460
have this expectation.

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:27.360
And this term here
is not something

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.920
we know how to deal with,
but you can probably

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:34.340
start to see that we are
somehow on the right track

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:37.820
because we know that whenever
we have some quantity that

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:41.760
is defined as expectations under
the trajectory distribution,

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:44.780
we can just approximate this
expectation through interactions

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:47.740
with the environment
and through sampling.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:49.680
So this is one step in
the right direction,

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.260
but still not
completely actionable

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:54.460
because we have this grad log p
in the equation, which is still

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:55.520
something we don't know.

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:03.300
So let's keep going.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:09.220
So let us recall as from the
previous slide, the definition

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:11.580
of the trajectory distribution,
which is basically

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.580
this factorization
of what for us

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:16.100
is the trajectory distribution.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:19.980
If we take the logarithm of
the trajectory distribution,

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:21.940
products become sums.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:24.280
Each individual terms
becomes the-- of course,

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:26.060
we take the logarithm.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:28.700
And basically, what
we get is the log

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:33.770
p's is equal to the sum of
these three terms over here.

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:36.270
So the logarithm of the
probability of the initial

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:40.950
state, plus the sum over time
of the log pi's, essentially

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.110
the logarithm of the
distribution induced

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:45.710
by our policy, and the log p's.

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:49.510
That is the transition dynamics.

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:54.390
So we just substitute this in
into our equation of the policy

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:59.210
gradient, and we get this
object over here, where--

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:04.830
yes, so we are taking
the gradient with respect

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:08.010
to theta of this
entire term here,

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:11.750
which we derived as the
logarithm of the probability

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:15.630
distribution over trajectories,
multiplied by the sum of rewards

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:18.910
in that trajectory.

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:20.910
Now, of course, notice
that since we're

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:24.470
taking the gradient
with respect to theta,

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:26.590
the first and the
third term here

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:29.050
don't depend on theta
directly, so they don't

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:30.790
contribute to our gradient.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:36.530
And notice that the
only term that remains

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:38.730
is actually something
that we know

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:44.690
how to compute because the
policy is an object we define.

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:48.810
For example, the
policy for us might

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:52.210
be a Gaussian over a
one-dimensional continuous

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:56.490
action, so we could
have our u of t.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:16:02.050
And so our policy
pi of ut given xt

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:06.010
might be a Gaussian, where
the parameters theta might

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:09.610
represent maybe the mean and
the variance of that Gaussian.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:16.030
And so basically, the log
pi's are completely specified,

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:17.630
and so we can actually
evaluate them.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:23.650
And so this means that if we
take that expression over there

00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:28.030
and just rewrite it
compactly over here,

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:30.990
where the only thing that
changes that we re-expanded

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:35.190
our terms small r of tau.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:39.270
We see that we get an expression
of the policy gradient, which

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.430
is something where
first of all, everything

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:44.050
inside this
expectation is known,

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:45.510
so this is just
the sum of rewards

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:49.790
that we observe according
to some trajectories tau.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:53.470
This is the sum of the log pi's
according to our policy, which,

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:55.910
again, is something we
know how to compute,

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:58.290
and so everything inside
this expectation is known.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:05.050
Do these steps make sense
or any questions until now?

00:17:13.845 --> 00:17:16.230
So we said that everything
inside this expectation

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:18.470
is known.

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:20.109
How do we evaluate
this in practice?

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:21.490
We use the same trick.

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:23.390
So we have this
expectation under

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:25.130
the trajectory distribution.

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:29.320
We know that we can approximate
this as the empirical mean

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:33.840
by basically sampling n
rollouts in our environment.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:35.580
We did this before
for the objective,

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:40.400
so we can do exactly the same
with the policy gradient, which

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:44.520
basically means that we can now
completely evaluate the policy

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:50.440
gradient that we're interested
in by taking n rollouts

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:55.840
or trajectories, basically
tau's in the environment,

00:17:55.840 --> 00:17:58.280
computing this quantity for
all of these trajectories,

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:00.575
and taking the mean.

00:18:00.575 --> 00:18:02.200
And this would be a
valid approximation

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:03.620
of the policy gradient.

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:21.480
And so basically,
if this is clear

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:26.320
through this derivation
of the policy gradient,

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:28.880
this leads us directly
into, if you wish,

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:31.180
the very first
policy optimization

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:35.100
algorithms that we will see in
this class, which is also known

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.580
as the reinforced algorithm.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:43.460
So as you can probably
imagine, the derivations

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:47.000
that we derived so far basically
give us an actionable strategy,

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:49.900
because now what we can do
is something along the lines

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:52.020
of these three steps over here.

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:55.660
So we will be sampling
n trajectories

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.660
from the environment by
basically just letting

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:01.020
our policy interact with
the environment itself.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.100
Through these
trajectories, we can now

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:06.700
always evaluate these gradient,
or the policy gradient,

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.820
or, at least, approximate it.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:12.540
And once we have this estimate
of the policy gradient,

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:14.860
we can take a step in
the parameter space,

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:19.380
according to this gradient,
to improve basically

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:22.830
the quality of our
parameters pi theta

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:24.330
that represent our policy pi.

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:32.990
So taking the bird's eye view
on this reinforce algorithm,

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:35.390
according to our usual
skeleton, kind of we

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:38.510
have our usual three
components, whereby

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.470
we are generating
samples by interacting

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.790
with the environment.

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:46.730
We are estimating our
return through Monte Carlo,

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:49.470
because we're basically running
our policy in the environment

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:52.110
and explicitly summing
up all of the rewards

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:55.710
that we observe at the
end of our episode,

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:58.790
and then we're using those
estimates of the returns

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:01.590
to improve the
policy, namely taking

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.090
a step in parameter space.

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:14.550
Of course, this is,
if you wish, one step

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:16.950
of an inner loop,
whereby we would then

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:18.650
have to repeat this over time.

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:20.990
So here, we're taking
one gradient step,

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:23.690
but then you can
imagine that we have

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:27.170
outer for loop that is kind
of wrapping these three

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:30.330
steps, where we repeat this
process because now we will have

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.490
an improved policy with some
new parameters theta, which will

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:36.210
collect new experience, and
we'll use this experience

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:39.510
to re-estimate the gradient
and keep iterating over time.

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:55.770
But let's stop for
a second and try

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:59.370
to build some intuition into
what policy gradient is actually

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:01.570
doing under the
hood and indeed, get

00:21:01.570 --> 00:21:06.850
some intuition behind the math,
which I think is always useful.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:11.490
So we did all of those
kind of algebraic steps

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:15.530
to basically derive this
estimator for the policy

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:16.370
gradient.

00:21:19.001 --> 00:21:23.060
We explained before how
the log pies essentially

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:32.500
represent the log
probabilities of the action

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:33.760
according to our policy.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.380
And we saw before how this
could be represented either

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:39.180
in the continuous space,
exactly as we were doing here

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:41.940
on the slide, but
this is equally

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:45.020
holds if we're looking at
a discrete action space,

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:48.140
whereby namely, the
policy will be expressing

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:52.580
a categorical distribution
over a finite set of actions

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:54.900
that we could pick.

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:58.140
And so this is basically
the term that we derived.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.540
But it's useful to compare
it with the expression

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:03.540
of the gradient
that we would when

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:05.980
we try to perform
maximum likelihood,

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:08.940
or if you wish, kind of
behavior cloning for us

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:11.060
for the purpose of control.

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:13.240
So remember the setting
of behavior cloning.

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:15.600
In behavior cloning, we
have some demonstrations

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:19.400
of an expert doing something
and executing a task, which

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:24.800
for us, namely means data sets
of tuples of states and action

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:28.960
pairs, which basically are
examples of this mapping

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:33.100
that we wish to approximate.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:36.100
In maximum likelihood
estimation or behavior cloning,

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:37.600
what we wanted to
do is, we wanted

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:41.080
to maximize the
probability of the actions

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:46.960
that we observed from our
expert given the state.

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:50.920
And so formally, the gradient
in the maximum likelihood case

00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:54.800
would essentially be
comprised of this term, where

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:59.320
we are trying to maximize the
probability of the actions

00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:02.600
in our data set because
this would essentially

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:05.720
mean be equivalent
to approximating

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:07.320
the behavior of the expert.

00:23:10.596 --> 00:23:15.700
If you compare this
equation of the gradient

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:18.680
to what we just derived
for the policy gradient,

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:22.860
you see that this is
essentially a weighted version

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:26.020
of the gradient that you would
get from maximum likelihood

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:30.820
estimation, where the weight is
specifically the sum of rewards

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:33.940
in that trajectory.

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:35.060
So what does this mean?

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:38.780
Is that in behavior
cloning, we taking a step

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:41.020
in the direction of the
gradient, essentially

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:45.500
means updating the probability
of all the actions in my data

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:47.820
set.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:51.020
In policy gradient, we are
updating the probability

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:54.140
of the actions according
to their performance

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:57.340
in those rollouts,
so those actions that

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:03.260
led to higher reward are more
updated in my policy gradient

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.960
term, and so we're
updating that probability,

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:06.800
if that makes sense.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:12.735
Is this clear?

00:24:12.735 --> 00:24:13.610
Does that make sense?

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:26.550
Indeed, and so the
policy gradient

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:29.190
is a weighted version of the
maximum likelihood estimation

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.790
gradient.

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:37.910
So again, to hammer
down this idea,

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:40.790
if we take a step in the
direction of the policy

00:24:40.790 --> 00:24:42.630
gradient, what we're
doing is that we're

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:45.990
changing the parameters
theta such that trajectories

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:49.550
with high reward have
higher probability.

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:54.630
And so you could imagine if we
have this fictitious landscape

00:24:54.630 --> 00:25:00.350
of rollouts, where
the width of the lines

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:02.510
here represents the
probability mass

00:25:02.510 --> 00:25:06.270
that my policy is allocating
to those trajectories.

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.990
Then here what we
want is we want

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:12.010
the good trajectories
to be more likely

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:13.650
than the bad trajectories.

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:17.450
And so in a way, policy
gradient really formalizes

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:19.950
this idea of learning
by trial and error,

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:24.210
because we are running out
kind of getting rollouts

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:26.550
in of the policy
in the environment,

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:28.930
so we're trying out
different strategies.

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:33.610
And then through basically
updates in parameter space,

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:37.410
we are learning what
is more effective

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:39.650
and making it more
likely, and we're

00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:42.310
learning what is less effective
and making it less likely.

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:56.550
And there's a lot of
imitation learning context.

00:25:56.550 --> 00:25:58.130
We could use this
approach because we

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:01.030
don't have an explicit
formulation of the reward.

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:03.450
Is that kind of why or?

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:05.735
I would say, so, yes.

00:26:05.735 --> 00:26:07.860
For example, you might not
have an explicit measure

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:09.260
of the reward.

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:12.740
I mean, you could construct
it and then maybe reweight

00:26:12.740 --> 00:26:15.840
your behavior cloning
loss, for example,

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:18.087
so this is something
that if you recall,

00:26:18.087 --> 00:26:20.420
we did discuss towards the
end of the behavioral cloning

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:22.940
class, where we looked at--

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:25.380
So we refer to them
as reinforcement

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:28.500
via supervised learning
methods, where we did

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:30.220
have a notion of
reward, and maybe we

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:33.780
use that to filter the
demonstrations or whatnot.

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:36.560
But so at face value, yes,
this is the reason why.

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:49.300
And so I would say, this is
probably the most fundamental

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:51.340
idea for this lecture.

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:56.123
So we started saying,
we want to approach

00:26:56.123 --> 00:26:57.540
the reinforcement
learning problem

00:26:57.540 --> 00:26:58.873
from a slightly different angle.

00:26:58.873 --> 00:27:01.220
That is one of
policy optimization,

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:03.740
so we parameterize
our policy, and then

00:27:03.740 --> 00:27:07.520
we cast reinforcement
learning as an optimization

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:11.240
problem with respect to these
parameters of the policy.

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:13.013
And to do this, we
do gradient ascent,

00:27:13.013 --> 00:27:14.680
and so everything
that we've done so far

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:18.040
is derive the expression
of the gradient.

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:21.660
So of course, this is not it.

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:24.920
There is much more to policy
optimization in the sense

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:26.580
that what we discussed
so far is great,

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:29.140
and from an
intuitive standpoint,

00:27:29.140 --> 00:27:33.120
it also makes a lot of sense,
at least, to me personally.

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:36.100
But I would say that if
you look at this gradient,

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:39.120
there are some clear
limitations, and I would say,

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:42.200
the main one is the fact
that this gradient, so

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:46.860
our estimate of the gradient,
has extremely high variance.

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:49.740
And so this is an
extremely, if you wish,

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:53.520
unstable learning
signal to follow.

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:57.320
But so let us now
spend a few minutes

00:27:57.320 --> 00:28:00.660
on this problem of high
variance of the policy gradient.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:05.250
And so we'll do now is,
we'll first briefly build

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:08.590
some intuition to why this high
variance might be happening,

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:10.330
and then we'll look
at two strategies

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:12.990
for reducing the variance
of the policy gradient.

00:28:17.170 --> 00:28:20.570
So consider this, again,
kind of fictitious example

00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:21.810
here on the slide.

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:25.490
Specifically, here
we are visualizing,

00:28:25.490 --> 00:28:29.910
if you wish, on the x-axis
the space of trajectories,

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:32.410
so all possible
trajectories that we may

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:36.490
follow according to our policy.

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:40.530
And this Gaussian here
is basically represents

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:44.830
a distribution over trajectories
induced by our policy pi.

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:52.190
Now, say that we basically run
our policy in the environment

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:55.350
and we observe
three trajectories,

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:58.630
and these three trajectories
have some associated return.

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:00.830
So we have these
three trajectories,

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:04.670
we sum up all of the rewards,
and we obtain this three

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:08.630
specific performances.

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:10.590
Now, out of these
three, two of them

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:15.910
were positive in this direction
and one of them was negative.

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:21.270
Now, from everything that we've
said so far on policy gradient,

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:24.630
well, we would expect
to policy gradient

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:27.850
to do is that if we then
use these trajectories,

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:30.430
estimate the gradient and
take a step in the parameter

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:34.030
space, what we're doing is that
we're making this behavior more

00:29:34.030 --> 00:29:36.550
likely than this behavior
here because this

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:39.790
is exactly what we've
been discussing so far.

00:29:39.790 --> 00:29:42.830
And this is actually
what would happen.

00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:46.230
But now let's suppose that
we take those same three

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:52.950
trajectories and simply add
the same positive constant

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:56.970
to the returns to get
these three new values.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:29:59.330
So the relative gap
is always the same,

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:01.850
but what changes is the
kind of absolute numbers

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:04.605
of those rewards.

00:30:04.605 --> 00:30:07.230
The catch here is that if we now
would do the exact same thing,

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:10.810
that is policy gradient on these
trajectories, what we would get

00:30:10.810 --> 00:30:13.410
is something slightly
more dispersed.

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:17.410
So because this is also a
positive number, in this case,

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:21.250
policy gradient
will still try to--

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:23.570
well, it will try to of
course, give more likelihood

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:25.530
to the better ones,
but it will still

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:30.730
try to cover this
trajectory over here.

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:34.050
And so, of course, this is, if
you wish, an extreme example,

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:36.850
but the main idea that
I'm trying to convey here

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:39.810
is that because
we are estimating

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:42.170
the true value of
the policy gradient

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:46.170
through samples, depending on
the samples that we observe,

00:30:46.170 --> 00:30:49.810
the policy gradient can vary
also quite substantially.

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:52.410
And so this is
exactly what we mean

00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:55.690
that the estimator that we came
up with of the policy gradient

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:59.300
has a high variance.

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:00.460
Why do we care about this?

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:03.780
Is because in practice,
if our estimator

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:06.980
of the gradient that we follow
for learning has high variance,

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:09.760
this will essentially
lead to worse performance,

00:31:09.760 --> 00:31:13.380
slower convergence, and
potentially more unstable

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:16.620
training curves, essentially.

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:20.780
So within this
framing, I would say

00:31:20.780 --> 00:31:24.460
that a lot, if not the
majority of the research

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:27.740
in policy optimization,
essentially revolves

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:32.340
around finding ways to lower the
variance of these estimators,

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:35.340
and so different algorithms
essentially boil down

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:38.520
to instantiating the policy
gradient in different ways.

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:47.700
Any questions on this?

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:49.340
Yeah.

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:52.820
So rewards can be discrete
or can be continuous.

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:57.680
And it's just like a more
clear gradient decrease

00:31:57.680 --> 00:31:59.810
the variance of
the policy gradient

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.440
is another interpretation?

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:02.720
Sorry.

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:07.020
Compared goal point let
say, and you reach the goal,

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:10.120
you get 5 points,
versus you get points

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:13.920
based on proximity to the goal,
so just continuous signal.

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:16.120
Just supplying a
continuous reward signal

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:18.120
decrease the variance?

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:21.960
Yes, I would say that
in general, the more

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:25.320
dense your reward
is, the better it

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:28.880
is for propagating that learning
throughout the algorithm,

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:30.467
in general.

00:32:30.467 --> 00:32:32.800
Now, it's not only because
of the variance of the policy

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:34.400
gradient, but I would
say, in general,

00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:37.500
also for credit assignment.

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:41.080
So if basically,
it encodes learning

00:32:41.080 --> 00:32:44.100
to be more granular throughout
your entire trajectory,

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:46.540
if that makes sense, but yes,
your intuition is correct.

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:56.410
So let's look at a
couple of strategies

00:32:56.410 --> 00:32:59.850
to reduce the variance
of policy gradients.

00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:04.330
So the first one, I would say,
is more than a simple trick

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:06.010
than an actual strategy.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:07.730
In the sense that
if we look back

00:33:07.730 --> 00:33:11.090
at what we were doing to
define the policy gradient,

00:33:11.090 --> 00:33:14.030
we essentially defined it as,
of course, the empirical mean,

00:33:14.030 --> 00:33:16.690
because we want to
approximate the expectations.

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:18.930
We have this first term,
which is the log pi's.

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:20.790
Great, something we can compute.

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:22.330
And we were weighting
the log pi's

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:26.970
by the sum of rewards throughout
the entire trajectory.

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:31.250
Now, if you look a little bit
more closely into what this is

00:33:31.250 --> 00:33:36.570
doing, is that what it may
happen that for time t prime,

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:42.130
greater than t, the log pi's
of this t prime would still be

00:33:42.130 --> 00:33:46.690
weighted by the sum of all
the t's before t prime,

00:33:46.690 --> 00:33:48.490
which is something
counterintuitive,

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:53.750
because basically the policy
at time t prime cannot really

00:33:53.750 --> 00:33:58.250
affect the reward for the
time steps before that action.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:02.850
And so this is what I mean by
causality here on the slide.

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:05.830
And so the first trick
essentially entails leveraging

00:34:05.830 --> 00:34:10.110
this intuition for the fact that
actions at time t prime cannot

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:12.510
affect the rewards
that happen before it,

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:16.270
and simply encode this into
how we are defining the policy

00:34:16.270 --> 00:34:19.270
gradient.

00:34:19.270 --> 00:34:22.909
So right now, here, I'm just
taking out this summation

00:34:22.909 --> 00:34:25.310
to make it more explicit.

00:34:25.310 --> 00:34:27.150
But so rather than
summing from t

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:29.710
equals 1, what we
would be doing here

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:36.909
is summing from t prime
equals t, specifically.

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:40.070
So this makes sense intuitively,
but why does this lower

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:40.695
the variance?

00:34:40.695 --> 00:34:42.070
So first of all,
of course, we're

00:34:42.070 --> 00:34:45.989
not including a lot of terms on
which the action doesn't really

00:34:45.989 --> 00:34:47.750
have any influence on.

00:34:47.750 --> 00:34:52.320
And then also, I would
say quite intuitively,

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:55.699
we are summing less terms
in our policy gradient.

00:34:55.699 --> 00:34:58.160
Less terms of the reward.

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:00.120
And remember that the
reward is essentially

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:04.760
something that depends on a
number of stochastic elements

00:35:04.760 --> 00:35:06.180
throughout the trajectory.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.920
So again, throughout
the trajectory,

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:11.880
we will be sampling repeatedly
from our policy, which

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.800
is indeed something stochastic.

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:15.920
We'll be sampling repeatedly
from the transition

00:35:15.920 --> 00:35:18.480
function, which is also
something stochastic.

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:22.460
And so simply put, here
we're summing less terms,

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:25.680
and so there is less variance
associated with this policy

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:27.140
gradient, overall.

00:35:32.225 --> 00:35:33.100
Does this make sense?

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:35.160
This is pretty simple
trick, I would say,

00:35:35.160 --> 00:35:38.660
but it does indeed
measurably improve things.

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:46.600
The second strategy,
which I would

00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:52.500
say is very important for the
space of policy optimization

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:57.180
methods, is one of introducing
the concept of baselines.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:36:00.680
So to understand exactly
what we mean by baseline,

00:36:00.680 --> 00:36:03.220
let's reconsider the
intuition we were discussing

00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:06.220
before regarding
policy gradient,

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:08.780
when we were saying that
policy gradient makes

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:12.420
good behavior more likely.

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:17.260
And so, specifically, what
we're discussing is that policy

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:21.420
gradient will only
do, quote-unquote,

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:24.820
"the right thing,"
if the returns,

00:36:24.820 --> 00:36:28.100
that is the sum of all the
rewards that we are observing,

00:36:28.100 --> 00:36:29.280
were centered.

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:32.660
So we specifically consider
this counter example

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:35.640
on the right, where the relative
difference was the same,

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:37.760
but simply those returns
were not centered,

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:40.060
and so the policy gradient
was actually doing something

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:43.035
that we didn't want it to do.

00:36:43.035 --> 00:36:44.660
Intuitively, what we
would like to have

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:49.400
instead is that we would like
to use some mechanism to center

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:53.920
our returns, such that the
policy gradient essentially

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:58.040
becomes something whereby the
probability of behavior that

00:36:58.040 --> 00:37:00.900
is better than average
gets increased,

00:37:00.900 --> 00:37:04.880
and the probability of behavior
that is worse than average gets

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:06.960
decreased.

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:09.440
So in other words, we
want to have a mechanism

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:12.040
to recenter the returns
from our interactions

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:15.520
with the environment.

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:17.680
The way we'll do this
is through something

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:19.440
that is referred to
as a baseline, here

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:22.600
referred to as b, which
is basically a quantity

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:26.480
that we are subtracting
to the sum of reward

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:28.180
to the return r of tau.

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:44.690
So before we get into actionable
ways of using the baseline,

00:37:44.690 --> 00:37:47.050
kind of a valuable
sanity check here

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:48.650
is so we're
introducing something

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:51.730
in the policy gradient.

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:55.010
We don't want to change the
expectation of the policy

00:37:55.010 --> 00:38:00.030
gradient because we derived
our equations analytically.

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:02.890
And we know that in
expectation, the policy gradient

00:38:02.890 --> 00:38:06.330
will go to the limit
of infinite samples

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:09.470
from our distribution
over trajectories,

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:11.490
we'll get the correct object.

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.850
But now if we subtract
this baseline b,

00:38:13.850 --> 00:38:17.110
do we still get the
same expectation?

00:38:17.110 --> 00:38:21.050
Is the estimator still
unbiased, essentially?

00:38:21.050 --> 00:38:23.330
And so the claim
here as a spoiler

00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:25.690
is that adding this
baseline does not

00:38:25.690 --> 00:38:28.570
change the value of
the expected gradient,

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:31.390
and so let's look at this
more and more closely.

00:38:34.210 --> 00:38:37.610
So to understand
this, let's consider

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:39.730
the following expectation.

00:38:39.730 --> 00:38:48.070
So specifically, let's look
at the part of the policy

00:38:48.070 --> 00:38:50.150
gradient, which only
takes into account

00:38:50.150 --> 00:38:55.830
the baseline, so the grad log
p is times the baseline p.

00:38:55.830 --> 00:38:58.250
Again, from the definition
of the expectation,

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:00.630
we can rewrite this
as the integral

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:06.850
of p tau, times the grad
log p, times the baseline b,

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:11.350
so here just the definition
of expectation again.

00:39:11.350 --> 00:39:14.470
And now we can use the
exact same identity

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:16.710
that we used before in the
derivation of the policy

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:20.110
gradient, but now in the
reverse direction, if you wish.

00:39:20.110 --> 00:39:27.310
So we can basically take
the p times grad log p's,

00:39:27.310 --> 00:39:33.430
which is equal to the
grad p's over here.

00:39:33.430 --> 00:39:36.850
And so this entails two grad
p's times the baseline b.

00:39:39.640 --> 00:39:43.440
Now, we can take the
b because this is just

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:46.040
a constant out of the integral.

00:39:46.040 --> 00:39:49.720
We can do the same with
the gradient operator,

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:53.960
and so what we get
is the b multiplied

00:39:53.960 --> 00:39:57.240
by the gradient of the integral
of the probability of tau

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:58.720
in the tau.

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:01.360
Since the probability
distribution over trajectories

00:40:01.360 --> 00:40:03.640
is a valid probability
density, we

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:07.240
know that the integral
has to be equal to 1.

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:10.760
And so what we get is we
get b, times the gradient

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:13.140
of a constant,
which is equal to 0.

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:22.560
And so this basically
makes the estimate

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:26.800
of the gradient with the
additional baseline term still

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:29.560
unbiased in expectation
because here,

00:40:29.560 --> 00:40:33.080
we basically verified
that the expected value

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:36.600
of the term that we are
subtracting is equal to 0,

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:39.100
and so we're not really
changing the actual expectation

00:40:39.100 --> 00:40:49.100
of our estimator, which
is something pretty

00:40:49.100 --> 00:40:51.740
neat because basically
now we have this mechanism

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:54.660
through which we can recenter
the returns in our policy

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:58.960
gradient, and therefore, reduce
the variance basically for free,

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:01.140
because we're not really
losing anything in terms

00:41:01.140 --> 00:41:06.100
of bias of our estimator.

00:41:06.100 --> 00:41:09.340
Since what we say is that
the goal here is to center

00:41:09.340 --> 00:41:14.300
the returns, a pretty
effective and popular choice

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:16.580
of the baseline is actually
to define the baseline

00:41:16.580 --> 00:41:18.420
as the average return.

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:21.860
So remember, again,
here we are interacting

00:41:21.860 --> 00:41:25.620
n times with our environments,
so we have n trajectories tile.

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:28.540
For all of these trajectories,
we will have some return,

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:31.580
and so we just define
b as the average return

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:33.120
over our trajectories.

00:41:35.950 --> 00:41:38.150
We'll see how this
idea of average returns

00:41:38.150 --> 00:41:40.790
basically motivates many
popular reinforcement learning

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:43.030
algorithms, but for
now, I would say,

00:41:43.030 --> 00:41:46.190
this is the intuition that
we are trying to capture.

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:50.250
So remember what
we're doing here.

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:55.070
We are interested in using
policy gradient like algorithms.

00:41:55.070 --> 00:41:58.630
We recognized that the policy
gradient is a high variance

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:02.310
object, and so we want to reduce
this variance through baselines.

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:05.610
And here, we basically
showed one way of doing so.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:14.650
Any questions on this?

00:42:24.418 --> 00:42:26.810
What does it mean in
practice to have a baseline?

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:30.390
Again, this is just kind of
a simple example of training

00:42:30.390 --> 00:42:33.190
curves, but to give you an idea
is that in practice what this

00:42:33.190 --> 00:42:36.770
would mean is that even for
very simple environments,

00:42:36.770 --> 00:42:42.050
you would get a
much faster training

00:42:42.050 --> 00:42:44.490
for the exact same
algorithm when

00:42:44.490 --> 00:42:46.650
you're just changing the
expression of the policy

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:47.450
gradient.

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:50.050
So here, we're
basically looking at it

00:42:50.050 --> 00:42:52.430
from the perspective of
maybe sample efficiency,

00:42:52.430 --> 00:42:54.850
so you converge faster,
but in some cases

00:42:54.850 --> 00:42:56.610
this actually makes
the difference

00:42:56.610 --> 00:43:01.675
between converging or not
converging to a good solution.

00:43:01.675 --> 00:43:03.050
So basically, this
is what I mean

00:43:03.050 --> 00:43:07.530
when I say that the quality
of the estimator of the policy

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:11.550
gradient actually influences
training quite substantially.

00:43:17.930 --> 00:43:23.510
So let's take a step back and
think about what we're doing.

00:43:23.510 --> 00:43:25.810
So everything that
we've done so far today

00:43:25.810 --> 00:43:29.010
is basically defining
a different way

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:31.940
of solving the reinforcement
learning objective.

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:36.740
First of all, we express it as a
function of the parameters theta

00:43:36.740 --> 00:43:40.760
of our policy, we evaluate the
gradient of that objective,

00:43:40.760 --> 00:43:44.460
we saw how to do it, and we
take a step in parameter space

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:46.500
according to this gradient.

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:49.660
Now, given basically this
high level structure,

00:43:49.660 --> 00:43:52.580
if you recall last
week, we were discussing

00:43:52.580 --> 00:43:55.940
the differences between on
and off policy algorithms.

00:43:55.940 --> 00:43:59.220
Specifically, we mentioned
that an on policy algorithm

00:43:59.220 --> 00:44:03.140
is an algorithm that
uses experience collected

00:44:03.140 --> 00:44:07.820
under the policy that we are
using to explore the environment

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:10.660
to improve.

00:44:10.660 --> 00:44:14.140
Would you say that this is an on
policy or off policy algorithm

00:44:14.140 --> 00:44:16.500
in this case?

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:17.400
Yeah.

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:21.220
[INAUDIBLE] are
updating the policy

00:44:21.220 --> 00:44:24.460
under different
runs of the market?

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:25.440
Yeah, exactly.

00:44:25.440 --> 00:44:29.100
So this is in this
specific framework,

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:32.880
as we've have seen so far,
policy gradient algorithms

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:35.120
are indeed a
naturally on policy,

00:44:35.120 --> 00:44:39.600
because the estimate
of the gradient

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:42.720
depends on basically,
the rollouts that we

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:46.840
did in the environment,
according to the current policy

00:44:46.840 --> 00:44:48.160
pi theta.

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:50.400
The moment we update
the policy, we'll

00:44:50.400 --> 00:44:52.280
have to collect new
experience in order

00:44:52.280 --> 00:44:56.020
to re-evaluate the policy
gradient for that policy.

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:04.300
Of course, there are extensions
of policy optimization.

00:45:04.300 --> 00:45:07.140
So the takeaway here is
not policy optimization.

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:09.720
Algorithms are on
policy, but there

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:12.260
are for the way we
derive it so far,

00:45:12.260 --> 00:45:14.700
these are indeed on
policy algorithms.

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:20.960
And indeed, exactly
as we said, it

00:45:20.960 --> 00:45:24.480
is on policy because we need
samples from the current policy

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:27.080
to estimate the gradient.

00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:29.460
So let's look a little
bit more closely

00:45:29.460 --> 00:45:35.500
at a couple of pros and cons
of policy gradient methods.

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:40.860
First of all, it's
nice to point out

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:44.700
how policy gradient
methods naturally handle

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:46.660
continuous action spaces.

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:49.740
And this is because
basically, all we have to do

00:45:49.740 --> 00:45:52.700
is we have to define an
appropriate representation

00:45:52.700 --> 00:45:55.800
of our policy pi of ut given xt.

00:45:55.800 --> 00:45:57.780
For example, this
could be a Gaussian,

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:00.960
and we parameterize the
parameters of that Gaussian.

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:06.540
So same thing if we were to
use a discrete distribution, so

00:46:06.540 --> 00:46:08.700
a categorical
distribution, we will

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:11.580
be essentially able to leverage
the exact same formalism

00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:16.500
regardless if the action space
is continuous or discrete.

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:19.080
On the other hand, in
value-based methods,

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:23.060
if you recall, the policy for
us was basically, implicitly

00:46:23.060 --> 00:46:29.250
defined as an argmax over you
of, for example, a q function.

00:46:32.742 --> 00:46:36.910
Doing this argmax is obviously
easy in the moment in which this

00:46:36.910 --> 00:46:40.310
is the action space is
discrete, so we have finite set

00:46:40.310 --> 00:46:42.710
of values for our actions u.

00:46:42.710 --> 00:46:45.550
The moment u is a
continuous object, then

00:46:45.550 --> 00:46:47.910
this in itself is an
optimization problem,

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:50.490
so which might or
might not be trivial.

00:46:53.070 --> 00:46:54.210
Same caveat here.

00:46:54.210 --> 00:46:56.390
There are, of
course, methods that

00:46:56.390 --> 00:47:00.390
allow value-based methods to be
applied for continuous action

00:47:00.390 --> 00:47:04.270
spaces, but at face value,
this is a clear distinction.

00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:13.830
A second pro is that
by how we derived it,

00:47:13.830 --> 00:47:16.470
policy optimization
methods directly

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:18.970
optimize the objective
we care about,

00:47:18.970 --> 00:47:21.950
which is the discounted
sum of rewards over time.

00:47:21.950 --> 00:47:26.210
So we are clearly
differentiating

00:47:26.210 --> 00:47:28.250
between policy
optimization methods that

00:47:28.250 --> 00:47:32.570
take the rl objective and
optimize this objective at face

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:34.850
value, so we literally
do gradient ascent

00:47:34.850 --> 00:47:36.530
on that objective.

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:39.730
From value-based methods
such as q-learning,

00:47:39.730 --> 00:47:44.250
which derive a mechanism, for
example, the Bellman optimality

00:47:44.250 --> 00:47:49.170
equations, to enforce a kind
of a fixed point iteration

00:47:49.170 --> 00:47:52.090
scheme that at
convergence will also

00:47:52.090 --> 00:47:55.130
lead to optimizing
that same objective,

00:47:55.130 --> 00:47:58.970
but for intermediate solutions,
it's not really clear

00:47:58.970 --> 00:48:00.890
if there is a partial ordering.

00:48:00.890 --> 00:48:03.070
So for policy
optimization method,

00:48:03.070 --> 00:48:07.250
since we're doing hill
climbing, if we stop halfway,

00:48:07.250 --> 00:48:10.290
we probably have a good
estimate of how much we

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:12.330
have been improving over time.

00:48:12.330 --> 00:48:14.650
On the other hand,
with q-learning, we

00:48:14.650 --> 00:48:16.785
know that we have
guarantees of convergence,

00:48:16.785 --> 00:48:18.410
but if we stop halfway,
we don't really

00:48:18.410 --> 00:48:22.940
know how we are with
respect to previous versions

00:48:22.940 --> 00:48:25.080
of the policies we computed.

00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:29.500
Yes.

00:48:29.500 --> 00:48:33.140
Sir, what do you mean by, for
q-learning, to stop halfway?

00:48:33.140 --> 00:48:36.060
Yes, so in both cases,
remember that we're

00:48:36.060 --> 00:48:39.240
looking at sequential
iterative algorithms.

00:48:39.240 --> 00:48:41.580
So here, this is
basically gradient ascent,

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:43.900
which we take sequential steps.

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:47.080
In q-learning, we are basically
doing fixed point iteration,

00:48:47.080 --> 00:48:50.260
so we are recursively enforcing
maybe the Bellman optimality

00:48:50.260 --> 00:48:51.500
equation.

00:48:51.500 --> 00:48:57.420
In both cases, you might
stop before convergence.

00:48:57.420 --> 00:48:59.700
In policy optimization
case, I would

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:02.340
argue it's pretty clear
to understand progress

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:05.180
because you're
maximizing an objective.

00:49:05.180 --> 00:49:07.260
In q-learning,
it's less clear how

00:49:07.260 --> 00:49:10.380
to value, to compute,
or evaluate progress

00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:13.400
in between before you
reach convergence.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:21.240
But I would say,
the bottom line here

00:49:21.240 --> 00:49:23.000
is that in value-based
learning, we

00:49:23.000 --> 00:49:27.360
are introducing an intermediate
objective to achieve

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:29.480
the same thing, while
in policy optimization,

00:49:29.480 --> 00:49:33.260
we say we want to optimize the
rl problem and we just do that.

00:49:37.040 --> 00:49:41.640
The con is that at face value,
at least, the kind of algorithms

00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:46.160
we just discussed are on policy
compared to, for example,

00:49:46.160 --> 00:49:50.040
q-learning, which might be prone
to off policy learning as well.

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:54.040
And so on policy in this
case can be extremely sample

00:49:54.040 --> 00:49:57.120
inefficient, whereby
we essentially,

00:49:57.120 --> 00:49:59.460
mean that we might have to
interact with the environment

00:49:59.460 --> 00:50:01.980
many, many times before
we actually converge.

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:12.940
Any questions so far before we
move on to actor-critic methods?

00:50:20.810 --> 00:50:25.970
And so let's now look at
actor critic methods, which

00:50:25.970 --> 00:50:29.650
build, I would say,
quite substantially

00:50:29.650 --> 00:50:31.850
on all the ideas we
introduced so far,

00:50:31.850 --> 00:50:34.650
namely the policy gradient.

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:37.610
But I would say, at their
core, actor-critic methods

00:50:37.610 --> 00:50:43.130
try to blend and leverage
the best of both worlds

00:50:43.130 --> 00:50:47.310
between policy gradient methods
and value-based methods,

00:50:47.310 --> 00:50:49.470
and we'll see shortly how.

00:50:53.890 --> 00:50:56.490
So specifically, consider,
again, the policy

00:50:56.490 --> 00:50:58.850
gradient expression
we just derived,

00:50:58.850 --> 00:51:01.990
which is, again, the sum.

00:51:01.990 --> 00:51:05.050
So the average of the
sum of the log pi's,

00:51:05.050 --> 00:51:09.770
times the sum of the
reward of our trajectory.

00:51:09.770 --> 00:51:13.490
The sum of rewards from
time t up until capital T,

00:51:13.490 --> 00:51:15.250
is also referred
to as the reward

00:51:15.250 --> 00:51:17.230
to go, because
essentially, encodes

00:51:17.230 --> 00:51:19.110
all of the rewards
that we will be

00:51:19.110 --> 00:51:22.810
seeing in the future
of our trajectory.

00:51:27.990 --> 00:51:31.270
And in a way basically,
all of the discussions

00:51:31.270 --> 00:51:33.430
of the high variance
of the policy

00:51:33.430 --> 00:51:35.950
gradient that we were doing
before, essentially boil

00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:42.510
down that to the fact
that this reward to go

00:51:42.510 --> 00:51:46.150
is indeed the part
whereby we have

00:51:46.150 --> 00:51:48.390
that is basically contributing
to this high variance

00:51:48.390 --> 00:51:50.990
because this reward
to go, we only

00:51:50.990 --> 00:51:57.030
have a limited sample estimate
of what the expected reward

00:51:57.030 --> 00:51:59.710
to go is.

00:51:59.710 --> 00:52:02.690
And when we actually sample
from the environment,

00:52:02.690 --> 00:52:06.750
this might only be kind of a
noisy estimate of the ground

00:52:06.750 --> 00:52:10.390
truth expectation
we're interested in.

00:52:10.390 --> 00:52:12.670
Why is this a noisy estimate?

00:52:12.670 --> 00:52:14.710
Is because, again, as
we were saying before,

00:52:14.710 --> 00:52:16.730
we are basically
repeatedly sampling

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:19.170
from various sources
of noise, and so even

00:52:19.170 --> 00:52:22.210
if we were to take the exact
same sequence of actions,

00:52:22.210 --> 00:52:25.690
over time, we might end in
slightly different rollouts

00:52:25.690 --> 00:52:27.510
in our environment.

00:52:30.250 --> 00:52:32.810
So I would say,
actor-critic methods

00:52:32.810 --> 00:52:37.570
are kind of fundamentally
say the following.

00:52:37.570 --> 00:52:43.130
So rather than estimating
these expected rewards

00:52:43.130 --> 00:52:46.210
to go through sampling,
whereby sampling

00:52:46.210 --> 00:52:48.610
basically entails just
evaluating rollouts

00:52:48.610 --> 00:52:50.770
of our policy and
the environment,

00:52:50.770 --> 00:52:56.770
why don't we try to estimate the
actual expectation by fitting,

00:52:56.770 --> 00:53:02.030
for example, a
parametric function,

00:53:02.030 --> 00:53:04.290
to basically estimate
this ground truth

00:53:04.290 --> 00:53:08.090
expectation that we care about?

00:53:08.090 --> 00:53:12.340
And so at a high level,
what actor-critic methods do

00:53:12.340 --> 00:53:15.540
is that they define two
parametric functions that we

00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:17.180
are interested in learning.

00:53:17.180 --> 00:53:19.460
The first one is the actor.

00:53:19.460 --> 00:53:22.340
Basically, our policy, so
our policy pi of theta.

00:53:22.340 --> 00:53:24.140
So we have some
parameters theta that we

00:53:24.140 --> 00:53:26.500
wish to estimate as
we're doing so far,

00:53:26.500 --> 00:53:30.420
but they also introduce a critic
that is basically a mechanism

00:53:30.420 --> 00:53:35.860
to estimate more accurately
the expected reward to go

00:53:35.860 --> 00:53:38.300
that we would like to have.

00:53:38.300 --> 00:53:40.200
How do we do this?

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:41.700
This is basically
everything that we

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:45.380
have been doing so far in
the last couple of weeks,

00:53:45.380 --> 00:53:50.380
which is basically, we want to
estimate the value functions.

00:53:50.380 --> 00:53:54.700
And so we saw before
already how we can use,

00:53:54.700 --> 00:53:56.940
for example, parametric
approximations

00:53:56.940 --> 00:54:01.820
to estimate an approximate
value functions.

00:54:01.820 --> 00:54:06.620
So very high level, what
actor-critic methods do

00:54:06.620 --> 00:54:11.440
is that they recognize that
part of this high variance

00:54:11.440 --> 00:54:13.920
problem that we see
in policy gradients

00:54:13.920 --> 00:54:19.520
basically is caused by
approximating the reward

00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:21.760
to go through samples.

00:54:21.760 --> 00:54:25.660
And so instead of approximating
this reward to go to samples,

00:54:25.660 --> 00:54:30.000
let's fit a parametric
approximator of the ground truth

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:34.880
expectation, which assuming
we are able to do correctly,

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:39.800
then this will reduce the
variance of my policy gradient.

00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:43.960
So putting this together,
instead of multiplying my log

00:54:43.960 --> 00:54:48.180
pi's by the sample return
as we were doing over there,

00:54:48.180 --> 00:54:51.160
so our reward to go,
let's replace the reward

00:54:51.160 --> 00:54:55.600
to go with an estimator
of, for example,

00:54:55.600 --> 00:54:58.020
a value function with
some parameters phi.

00:55:07.930 --> 00:55:08.870
Does this make sense?

00:55:20.770 --> 00:55:24.490
And so if this is clear--

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:29.210
so this is a good
idea, so let's just fit

00:55:29.210 --> 00:55:33.930
a parametric function
to our value functions.

00:55:33.930 --> 00:55:37.170
Can we combine what we're
doing, so this idea,

00:55:37.170 --> 00:55:41.610
with the discussion on
baselines that we did before?

00:55:41.610 --> 00:55:43.250
Well, the answer is yes.

00:55:43.250 --> 00:55:46.690
So let us try to reduce
even further the variance

00:55:46.690 --> 00:55:51.410
of our policy gradient
by basically taking

00:55:51.410 --> 00:55:55.650
our new estimate of the
policy gradient, where

00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:58.290
we are using our
fitted q function,

00:55:58.290 --> 00:56:00.590
and let us add the
baseline again.

00:56:03.610 --> 00:56:05.330
Exactly as we were
saying before,

00:56:05.330 --> 00:56:07.950
the intuition that we tried
to leverage in the baseline

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:11.590
is that we wanted to
center our returns.

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:19.130
And so in this case, to center
our value function here,

00:56:19.130 --> 00:56:22.610
we could pick a state
dependent baseline b of xt.

00:56:22.610 --> 00:56:28.150
That depends on state xt, which
is basically the expected q

00:56:28.150 --> 00:56:29.390
function value.

00:56:29.390 --> 00:56:31.690
So here, we're basically
doing the same thing.

00:56:31.690 --> 00:56:34.390
We want to center this quantity.

00:56:34.390 --> 00:56:35.810
How do we center this?

00:56:35.810 --> 00:56:39.550
Well, we could pick b as the
expected q function, where

00:56:39.550 --> 00:56:44.990
this expectation is taken under
the distribution over controls

00:56:44.990 --> 00:56:47.090
ut according to our policy.

00:56:53.470 --> 00:57:01.110
So even if the exact details
here are slightly unclear,

00:57:01.110 --> 00:57:05.440
I would say, the main idea
here that we want to take home

00:57:05.440 --> 00:57:09.320
is that actor-critic
methods introduce

00:57:09.320 --> 00:57:10.840
an estimate of
the value function

00:57:10.840 --> 00:57:13.040
to better approximate
the reward to go.

00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:14.080
This is great.

00:57:14.080 --> 00:57:16.640
Can we combine this
idea with baselines?

00:57:16.640 --> 00:57:19.800
Yes, we can do that.

00:57:19.800 --> 00:57:23.280
We said that with baselines, we
wanted to recenter our reward

00:57:23.280 --> 00:57:25.460
to go term.

00:57:25.460 --> 00:57:28.040
We can center this by picking
the baseline in this way,

00:57:28.040 --> 00:57:29.240
essentially.

00:57:37.240 --> 00:57:42.160
Now, let's look at this term
a little bit more closely.

00:57:42.160 --> 00:57:47.800
The expected q function
under our policy

00:57:47.800 --> 00:57:52.640
is basically nothing else that
the value function itself.

00:57:52.640 --> 00:57:58.360
So this is something that can
be somewhat confusing at first,

00:57:58.360 --> 00:58:01.900
but let us try to
look a little bit more

00:58:01.900 --> 00:58:03.080
closely with an example.

00:58:03.080 --> 00:58:08.340
So consider a discrete
environment, again, for example

00:58:08.340 --> 00:58:11.780
our gridworld, where
we can take maybe one

00:58:11.780 --> 00:58:14.280
of a finite number of actions.

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:23.380
So we know that the q
function, in this case,

00:58:23.380 --> 00:58:33.700
under some policy pi is
basically the expected reward

00:58:33.700 --> 00:58:37.500
that we expect to have
in state x, taking action

00:58:37.500 --> 00:58:43.220
ut, and then following the
policy pi for all the time

00:58:43.220 --> 00:58:44.040
steps that come.

00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:47.800
So this is the definition that
we had so far of the q function.

00:58:51.620 --> 00:58:54.580
Where specifically you see
that here we are basically

00:58:54.580 --> 00:58:57.260
picking the action ut
because the q function

00:58:57.260 --> 00:59:01.840
is defined over this
tuple, states and controls.

00:59:01.840 --> 00:59:03.720
How can we connect
the q function

00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:09.400
with the value function
for that same policy?

00:59:09.400 --> 00:59:13.560
Is that intuitively,
the value of a state

00:59:13.560 --> 00:59:19.160
is the average
value that we would

00:59:19.160 --> 00:59:25.240
get of the future states,
where this average is taken

00:59:25.240 --> 00:59:29.080
under the probability
distribution that

00:59:29.080 --> 00:59:32.840
decides which action I'm picking
right now, if that makes sense.

00:59:32.840 --> 00:59:38.040
So this value function
here is basically

00:59:38.040 --> 00:59:41.280
averaging out the
future values, according

00:59:41.280 --> 00:59:44.860
to all the possible controls
that I might pick in that state.

00:59:49.920 --> 00:59:51.560
Does this make sense?

00:59:51.560 --> 00:59:52.080
Yeah.

00:59:52.080 --> 00:59:54.840
So it that-- now we can
cancel the [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:54.840 --> 00:59:58.850
to where you're looking
over all possible states

00:59:58.850 --> 01:00:01.430
are up for actions
in the future,

01:00:01.430 --> 01:00:04.690
and then, like you just
said, averaging them out?

01:00:04.690 --> 01:00:06.910
Yes, I would say the
intuition is very similar.

01:00:06.910 --> 01:00:10.510
So maybe one other way to
see this is a temporary.

01:00:10.510 --> 01:00:13.370
So basically, the only
thing that changes

01:00:13.370 --> 01:00:15.290
is the one step
reasoning that we're

01:00:15.290 --> 01:00:19.050
doing, because from
t plus 1 onwards,

01:00:19.050 --> 01:00:21.030
we're still sampling
from our policy,

01:00:21.030 --> 01:00:23.530
and so we're averaging out
over all possible things that

01:00:23.530 --> 01:00:26.210
could happen over our policy.

01:00:26.210 --> 01:00:29.450
In the one-step, so
from t to t plus 1,

01:00:29.450 --> 01:00:33.930
the q function is basically
has one information more, which

01:00:33.930 --> 01:00:37.530
is we are specifying what
control we're taking right now,

01:00:37.530 --> 01:00:42.570
so we're basically committing
to one specific action.

01:00:42.570 --> 01:00:44.690
On the other hand,
the value function

01:00:44.690 --> 01:00:47.450
is a function only of the state.

01:00:47.450 --> 01:00:49.250
And so what we're
basically saying

01:00:49.250 --> 01:00:53.490
is that the value of this state,
if we don't know which action

01:00:53.490 --> 01:00:55.330
we're picking in this
state, will basically

01:00:55.330 --> 01:00:58.670
depend on the average action
that we're picking in the state,

01:00:58.670 --> 01:01:07.670
and so the average action is
sampled according to our policy,

01:01:07.670 --> 01:01:08.610
if that makes sense.

01:01:11.950 --> 01:01:15.430
So this is just
basically a small detour,

01:01:15.430 --> 01:01:19.510
but useful to figure out
what we're saying here.

01:01:19.510 --> 01:01:23.470
So here, we're looking at
introducing the baselines.

01:01:23.470 --> 01:01:29.550
We mentioned that this was a
reasonable choice of a baseline.

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:32.830
We now just saw how the expected
q-function is nothing else

01:01:32.830 --> 01:01:35.430
than the value function.

01:01:35.430 --> 01:01:38.750
And so we can then rewrite
the expression of the policy

01:01:38.750 --> 01:01:42.950
gradient as, again, the
average over n trajectories

01:01:42.950 --> 01:01:46.910
of the grad log pi's,
multiplied by our critic

01:01:46.910 --> 01:01:50.670
estimator of the reward to go,
minus the value function, which

01:01:50.670 --> 01:01:53.250
in this case is the
policy is the baseline.

01:01:59.800 --> 01:02:02.040
And if you look at this
term a little bit more

01:02:02.040 --> 01:02:03.880
closely, what this
term is saying

01:02:03.880 --> 01:02:08.040
is that this is essentially
quantifying how much the action

01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:14.040
ut is better than the average
action in that same state xt.

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:16.240
And so following this
gradient essentially

01:02:16.240 --> 01:02:17.840
increases the
probability of actions

01:02:17.840 --> 01:02:20.920
that have returns
better than average

01:02:20.920 --> 01:02:24.280
and decreases the probability of
the actions that have returned

01:02:24.280 --> 01:02:28.040
worse than average,
so this is one way

01:02:28.040 --> 01:02:32.280
of recentering the returns.

01:02:32.280 --> 01:02:35.680
This quantity is actually
so important in practice

01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:37.440
that it has its
own name, and it is

01:02:37.440 --> 01:02:44.320
referred to as the
advantage function

01:02:44.320 --> 01:02:47.360
because it is as if this
quantity is encoding

01:02:47.360 --> 01:02:50.760
the advantage of taking
action u with respect

01:02:50.760 --> 01:02:54.320
to the average action
that I could take.

01:03:02.540 --> 01:03:03.400
Questions here.

01:03:06.260 --> 01:03:07.140
Yeah.

01:03:07.140 --> 01:03:12.140
The critic is like
agnostic of the actor.

01:03:12.140 --> 01:03:15.820
It's a model of actions
that you can take.

01:03:15.820 --> 01:03:20.540
It's you could swap out the
actor for a new one [INAUDIBLE],

01:03:20.540 --> 01:03:22.845
and it would still be
representative of the problem.

01:03:22.845 --> 01:03:24.220
Is that the right
interpretation?

01:03:24.220 --> 01:03:28.393
I would say so
functionally, this is just

01:03:28.393 --> 01:03:30.060
a separate parametric
function, so there

01:03:30.060 --> 01:03:31.460
are two different objects.

01:03:31.460 --> 01:03:35.540
Of course, the way we fit the
parameters of the critic, phi

01:03:35.540 --> 01:03:37.740
in this case,
depend on the policy

01:03:37.740 --> 01:03:44.300
because you can imagine that
we will be fitting our critic,

01:03:44.300 --> 01:03:47.040
depending on the experience
that we have in our environment,

01:03:47.040 --> 01:03:49.660
and this experience is
dictated by the policy.

01:03:49.660 --> 01:03:51.535
So I would say, this
is not independent,

01:03:51.535 --> 01:03:52.910
because here,
we're still fitting

01:03:52.910 --> 01:03:56.430
the value function
of that policy, which

01:03:56.430 --> 01:03:58.750
for us is the actor.

01:03:58.750 --> 01:04:00.230
Does that make sense?

01:04:00.230 --> 01:04:02.270
Yeah.

01:04:02.270 --> 01:04:05.190
I think a good example on
how policy might give you

01:04:05.190 --> 01:04:07.550
an average action and
what that might look

01:04:07.550 --> 01:04:10.390
like for the value function.

01:04:10.390 --> 01:04:15.030
So remember that
the policy for us

01:04:15.030 --> 01:04:16.970
is just a distribution
over actions.

01:04:16.970 --> 01:04:19.310
So in the continuous
case, it could

01:04:19.310 --> 01:04:22.990
be a Gaussian over a
continuous action ut,

01:04:22.990 --> 01:04:26.470
or in the discrete
case, it might be say,

01:04:26.470 --> 01:04:29.850
we have the four cardinal
directions again,

01:04:29.850 --> 01:04:32.190
and these are our actions.

01:04:32.190 --> 01:04:35.670
The policy might just be
a categorical distribution

01:04:35.670 --> 01:04:38.190
over these actions.

01:04:38.190 --> 01:04:43.950
So what I meant by average
action in all our discussions

01:04:43.950 --> 01:04:48.350
of value functions that are on
the slide was basically mean,

01:04:48.350 --> 01:04:53.830
since these are valid
distributions over actions,

01:04:53.830 --> 01:04:56.890
we could take the expectations
under these distributions,

01:04:56.890 --> 01:04:59.970
if that makes sense.

01:04:59.970 --> 01:05:05.730
So this is kind of a
stochastic environment?

01:05:05.730 --> 01:05:07.530
This would be a
stochastic policy.

01:05:07.530 --> 01:05:08.190
OK, yeah.

01:05:13.050 --> 01:05:15.410
Going back to this
question, but even

01:05:15.410 --> 01:05:18.650
if it will be a different
critic based on the actor,

01:05:18.650 --> 01:05:21.930
could you use a
pre-trained critic

01:05:21.930 --> 01:05:24.250
so you don't have to have
it start from scratch

01:05:24.250 --> 01:05:26.167
at the beginning of
training, or do you always

01:05:26.167 --> 01:05:27.150
train them together?

01:05:30.210 --> 01:05:32.030
So I have two answers.

01:05:32.030 --> 01:05:35.670
One answer is the kind of
theoretically grounded answer.

01:05:35.670 --> 01:05:40.090
I would say, no, you cannot
do that, because this is--

01:05:40.090 --> 01:05:42.232
so here, we're estimating
the value function.

01:05:42.232 --> 01:05:43.690
And remember, the
value function is

01:05:43.690 --> 01:05:47.070
an object that refers
to a specific policy,

01:05:47.070 --> 01:05:49.230
so here, we want
to use the value

01:05:49.230 --> 01:05:53.950
function of the policy
that we're learning,

01:05:53.950 --> 01:05:56.910
at least, for these algorithms
that we're looking at right now.

01:05:56.910 --> 01:06:04.230
In practice, as long
as this quantity here

01:06:04.230 --> 01:06:11.270
is something useful and aligned
for gradient updates, then

01:06:11.270 --> 01:06:14.410
you might get some benefit in
using a pre-trained critic.

01:06:17.750 --> 01:06:19.750
What's the one
specific mechanism

01:06:19.750 --> 01:06:23.510
that you're using to
update the phi parameters?

01:06:23.510 --> 01:06:25.970
Is it similar to q-learning
or is it something--

01:06:25.970 --> 01:06:26.573
Yeah, great.

01:06:26.573 --> 01:06:27.490
It's a great question.

01:06:27.490 --> 01:06:30.310
It's coming in one or two sides.

01:06:30.310 --> 01:06:32.470
Short answer is
if you recall what

01:06:32.470 --> 01:06:35.510
we were doing last week
with fitted q-learning

01:06:35.510 --> 01:06:40.050
or using function approximators
to estimate the value functions,

01:06:40.050 --> 01:06:42.390
this is exactly the
same thing, but it's

01:06:42.390 --> 01:06:43.850
coming in a couple of slides.

01:06:50.220 --> 01:06:56.880
And so we introduced actor
critic methods as strategies

01:06:56.880 --> 01:07:01.600
to try to blend the best of
both worlds between policy

01:07:01.600 --> 01:07:04.800
optimization and value-based
approaches, so let

01:07:04.800 --> 01:07:09.400
us even just visually look at an
example of a value-based methods

01:07:09.400 --> 01:07:12.120
in the top left, so
fitted q-learning

01:07:12.120 --> 01:07:17.480
and the visual version of a
policy optimization algorithm

01:07:17.480 --> 01:07:21.960
that is reinforce and compare
it with what we're doing here

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:23.520
with actor-critic methods.

01:07:23.520 --> 01:07:25.980
So in fitted q-learning,
if you remember,

01:07:25.980 --> 01:07:28.680
we were well, of course, running
policy in the environment

01:07:28.680 --> 01:07:31.000
to collect trajectories.

01:07:31.000 --> 01:07:36.200
And then we were fitting a
parametric function of our value

01:07:36.200 --> 01:07:41.080
function q phi in order to
basically solve a regression

01:07:41.080 --> 01:07:43.380
problem, so we
specifying some targets

01:07:43.380 --> 01:07:47.340
which could be either the Monte
Carlo targets or the TD target.

01:07:47.340 --> 01:07:50.620
And then we were trying to
minimize the prediction error

01:07:50.620 --> 01:07:53.900
from our parametric
approximation and the ground

01:07:53.900 --> 01:07:55.280
truth target.

01:08:00.620 --> 01:08:04.080
And this is exactly what we will
find in actor critic methods,

01:08:04.080 --> 01:08:06.780
so we still run the
policy in our environment

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:08.540
to generate trajectories.

01:08:08.540 --> 01:08:10.660
And here in actor
critic methods,

01:08:10.660 --> 01:08:13.740
we are fitting either a value
function, or a q function,

01:08:13.740 --> 01:08:15.080
or advantage function.

01:08:15.080 --> 01:08:15.920
It will depend.

01:08:15.920 --> 01:08:17.939
This is a choice,
but we are ideally

01:08:17.939 --> 01:08:19.979
doing exactly the same thing.

01:08:19.979 --> 01:08:22.220
So in a way, this is
exactly the same thing

01:08:22.220 --> 01:08:23.979
we're doing with
fitted q-learning.

01:08:23.979 --> 01:08:26.040
Here, we're doing
actor-critic methods.

01:08:28.740 --> 01:08:32.939
At the same time, while
in fitted q-learning,

01:08:32.939 --> 01:08:39.100
we were using the function to
implicitly define the policy.

01:08:39.100 --> 01:08:42.149
In actor-critic methods,
we are doing the same thing

01:08:42.149 --> 01:08:43.689
that we're doing
in the reinforce.

01:08:43.689 --> 01:08:47.710
That is, we are leveraging
our computations here

01:08:47.710 --> 01:08:51.069
to define a policy
gradient expression

01:08:51.069 --> 01:08:55.750
and use this to take a
step in parameter space.

01:08:55.750 --> 01:08:59.390
So I hope that, at least,
intuitively, you're

01:08:59.390 --> 01:09:03.350
starting to see how here we're
trying to stitch together

01:09:03.350 --> 01:09:06.270
different individual components
of different families

01:09:06.270 --> 01:09:07.410
of algorithms.

01:09:13.974 --> 01:09:15.689
Now, a question here is--

01:09:18.950 --> 01:09:22.390
so first of all, we
had many examples

01:09:22.390 --> 01:09:26.550
of using value functions v
using q functions or advantage

01:09:26.550 --> 01:09:27.670
functions.

01:09:27.670 --> 01:09:31.350
What quantity should we estimate
and what are the trade-offs?

01:09:31.350 --> 01:09:34.689
I would say, in general, there
are no right or wrong answers.

01:09:34.689 --> 01:09:36.189
I would say,
different algorithms

01:09:36.189 --> 01:09:38.170
might just have
different implementations

01:09:38.170 --> 01:09:42.850
of the same underlying idea.

01:09:42.850 --> 01:09:45.290
But for example, one thing
that you might consider

01:09:45.290 --> 01:09:49.569
is just the complexity of
the value function estimation

01:09:49.569 --> 01:09:50.609
problem.

01:09:50.609 --> 01:09:53.609
So for example,
the value function

01:09:53.609 --> 01:09:57.750
might be easier because it only
depends on states as the inputs.

01:09:57.750 --> 01:09:59.490
On the other hand,
if we were to learn

01:09:59.490 --> 01:10:02.210
a Q function or an
advantage function,

01:10:02.210 --> 01:10:05.450
we would have to learn a
mapping from states and controls

01:10:05.450 --> 01:10:06.630
rather than just states.

01:10:09.450 --> 01:10:14.410
I would say, probably the
most popular implementation

01:10:14.410 --> 01:10:19.850
of an actor-critic algorithm,
which uses the advantage

01:10:19.850 --> 01:10:24.330
also refers to as a to z,
or advantage actor-critic

01:10:24.330 --> 01:10:27.730
is something that has this
specific choice of what

01:10:27.730 --> 01:10:28.810
we're fitting.

01:10:28.810 --> 01:10:31.530
So in principle, we want
to use the advantage, which

01:10:31.530 --> 01:10:36.540
is the q function, minus
the value function v.

01:10:36.540 --> 01:10:38.220
Of course, what we
want to do is we

01:10:38.220 --> 01:10:41.380
want to avoid to have
to estimate two objects.

01:10:41.380 --> 01:10:44.900
We don't want to fit two
separate networks, one for the q

01:10:44.900 --> 01:10:47.020
function and one for
the value function.

01:10:47.020 --> 01:10:51.900
And so the trick that
is typically being used

01:10:51.900 --> 01:10:56.140
is that we can
approximate the Q function

01:10:56.140 --> 01:10:59.260
as the instantaneous
reward, plus the value

01:10:59.260 --> 01:11:02.180
function at the next state.

01:11:02.180 --> 01:11:06.060
And so according to
this approximation, now

01:11:06.060 --> 01:11:10.580
you can see how the
advantage can be rewritten

01:11:10.580 --> 01:11:13.900
as a function only of the value
function v of the state value

01:11:13.900 --> 01:11:16.300
function.

01:11:16.300 --> 01:11:21.640
And so basically, through this
kind of reparameterization here,

01:11:21.640 --> 01:11:24.540
this enables us to
basically fit the advantage

01:11:24.540 --> 01:11:28.540
or use the advantage in our
policy updates, while only

01:11:28.540 --> 01:11:31.680
having to fit the value
function, if that makes sense.

01:11:37.880 --> 01:11:41.880
And so indeed, the advantage
actor critic algorithm,

01:11:41.880 --> 01:11:45.640
or a to z is indeed
something that

01:11:45.640 --> 01:11:47.580
runs the policy in
the environment,

01:11:47.580 --> 01:11:52.720
uses those interactions
to fit a value function,

01:11:52.720 --> 01:11:56.640
uses that value function to
compute the advantage according

01:11:56.640 --> 01:12:04.240
to this approximation, and then
uses the advantage in the policy

01:12:04.240 --> 01:12:06.980
gradient expression.

01:12:19.260 --> 01:12:23.200
So now to answer also to
your colleague's question,

01:12:23.200 --> 01:12:26.120
so here, we're talking about
estimating different quantities

01:12:26.120 --> 01:12:29.160
and different value functions.

01:12:29.160 --> 01:12:30.580
How do we do this?

01:12:30.580 --> 01:12:35.130
And the main ideas
that we simply

01:12:35.130 --> 01:12:38.530
leverage exactly what we were
doing in value-based methods

01:12:38.530 --> 01:12:41.410
with function approximation.

01:12:41.410 --> 01:12:47.770
Specifically, you could do this
through a Monte Carlo learning.

01:12:47.770 --> 01:12:53.690
That is, we collect a data
set of state and return pairs,

01:12:53.690 --> 01:12:56.570
and basically, do
supervised learning

01:12:56.570 --> 01:12:59.030
on these tuples on these pairs.

01:13:02.690 --> 01:13:07.250
Or we could use equivalently,
TD learning by similarly,

01:13:07.250 --> 01:13:12.810
collecting a data set of
state and TD target pairs,

01:13:12.810 --> 01:13:15.450
and basically solve a
regression problem that

01:13:15.450 --> 01:13:19.090
fits a value function that
approximates, or minimizes

01:13:19.090 --> 01:13:21.110
the error with respect
to this target.

01:13:28.130 --> 01:13:30.410
Does this clarify?

01:13:30.410 --> 01:13:30.910
Yeah.

01:13:38.390 --> 01:13:43.090
OK, so now, just
as a brief detour.

01:13:46.270 --> 01:13:52.950
I wanted to briefly go
through the AlphaGo paper.

01:13:52.950 --> 01:13:59.070
First of all, to show you
that, this is like 10, 20, 14

01:13:59.070 --> 01:14:04.233
or something along
those times, but it

01:14:04.233 --> 01:14:06.650
is one of the biggest successes
of reinforcement learning,

01:14:06.650 --> 01:14:07.430
arguably.

01:14:07.430 --> 01:14:09.968
And I definitely invite
you to check out the paper,

01:14:09.968 --> 01:14:12.510
because I would say that through
everything that we discussed

01:14:12.510 --> 01:14:15.870
today, we actually can
understand, I would say,

01:14:15.870 --> 01:14:18.170
the entire paper most likely.

01:14:21.190 --> 01:14:23.050
And I just want to
highlight a couple of,

01:14:23.050 --> 01:14:24.630
I think interesting points.

01:14:24.630 --> 01:14:27.270
So remember, AlphaGo
is basically,

01:14:27.270 --> 01:14:31.570
this project, where they wanted
to learn through reinforcement

01:14:31.570 --> 01:14:35.290
learning how to
play Go, which is

01:14:35.290 --> 01:14:40.090
extremely high dimensional
board game, if you wish.

01:14:40.090 --> 01:14:46.285
The idea here was to
first of all, define--

01:14:46.285 --> 01:14:48.410
you already start seeing
a policy network and value

01:14:48.410 --> 01:14:53.730
network, so here, they
use an actor-critic method

01:14:53.730 --> 01:14:56.890
for this problem, whereby the
policy is basically something

01:14:56.890 --> 01:15:02.730
that maps states that is board
configurations to actions, which

01:15:02.730 --> 01:15:06.730
is a distribution
over a finite amount

01:15:06.730 --> 01:15:09.650
of next moves in the board.

01:15:09.650 --> 01:15:12.410
And the value
network is basically

01:15:12.410 --> 01:15:16.810
a parametric representation of
the state value function, which

01:15:16.810 --> 01:15:20.850
similarly maps from current
configuration of the board,

01:15:20.850 --> 01:15:24.130
or the state, to a
scalar number that

01:15:24.130 --> 01:15:28.980
represents the value of
that board configuration.

01:15:28.980 --> 01:15:38.860
Now, if we skim to how the
actual algorithm is implemented,

01:15:38.860 --> 01:15:41.620
so you will see how--

01:15:41.620 --> 01:15:43.580
So of course, we train
the policy network

01:15:43.580 --> 01:15:45.640
by policy gradient
reinforcement learning.

01:15:49.820 --> 01:15:54.080
It was learned
through self-play,

01:15:54.080 --> 01:15:59.620
so you could imagine having the
policy pitched against itself

01:15:59.620 --> 01:16:03.340
or previous versions of itself
in a simulated environment.

01:16:03.340 --> 01:16:07.220
And so the games were replayed
to determine the policy gradient

01:16:07.220 --> 01:16:11.720
update, which is defined
exactly as we were doing today.

01:16:11.720 --> 01:16:15.220
Again, the notation slightly
changes, but here, we

01:16:15.220 --> 01:16:17.660
are basically discussing our--

01:16:17.660 --> 01:16:20.260
this is the update that
you do in parameter space

01:16:20.260 --> 01:16:27.760
as the empirical mean 1/m times
a step size alpha of the-- here,

01:16:27.760 --> 01:16:32.640
we have the grad log pi's, so
the probability distribution

01:16:32.640 --> 01:16:37.160
over action given state,
times the z here represents

01:16:37.160 --> 01:16:40.680
the return, so the
return that they observed

01:16:40.680 --> 01:16:46.000
in the environment, minus the
value function of the state v.

01:16:46.000 --> 01:16:48.360
And here, they say
that indeed-- so they

01:16:48.360 --> 01:16:53.120
use the reinforce algorithm
with a baseline v of s, which

01:16:53.120 --> 01:16:55.780
is basically the value
function as a baseline,

01:16:55.780 --> 01:16:58.060
and they use this for
variance reduction.

01:17:00.680 --> 01:17:03.080
Again, I definitely
invite you to check this

01:17:03.080 --> 01:17:05.800
the details of this
paper, because I

01:17:05.800 --> 01:17:07.760
would say that vast
majority of the concepts

01:17:07.760 --> 01:17:10.920
here we did indeed cover today.

01:17:10.920 --> 01:17:14.260
Is there a reward signal we'll
use at the end of the episode?

01:17:14.260 --> 01:17:14.760
Yes.

01:17:18.880 --> 01:17:24.190
Of course, here we glossed over
one of the main contributions,

01:17:24.190 --> 01:17:29.330
which is the search
element of this approach,

01:17:29.330 --> 01:17:32.590
meaning in order to
sample from the policy,

01:17:32.590 --> 01:17:35.447
they weren't just sampling
from a categorical distribution

01:17:35.447 --> 01:17:37.530
of reaction, but they were
using that distribution

01:17:37.530 --> 01:17:40.970
to guide an explicit search
process, specifically

01:17:40.970 --> 01:17:43.650
Monte Carlo tree search.

01:17:43.650 --> 01:17:45.650
But I would say,
high-level intuitions

01:17:45.650 --> 01:17:48.210
from the perspective of
the reinforcement learning

01:17:48.210 --> 01:17:52.470
side of things are exactly
the topics we covered today.

01:17:58.470 --> 01:18:00.230
Yeah.

01:18:00.230 --> 01:18:01.730
There was nothing
in the policy they

01:18:01.730 --> 01:18:05.850
found that guaranteed to be
best, or was it like a human

01:18:05.850 --> 01:18:06.850
could not beat it?

01:18:06.850 --> 01:18:11.090
No, so, of course, the moment
you go for deep reinforcement

01:18:11.090 --> 01:18:13.770
learning algorithms,
there is no theory

01:18:13.770 --> 01:18:18.010
that can back any of
this in terms of proofs

01:18:18.010 --> 01:18:21.870
or guarantees of convergence.

01:18:21.870 --> 01:18:23.850
They were able to beat
the world champion Go,

01:18:23.850 --> 01:18:28.730
and so they claim this as a good
enough result for this policy.

01:18:28.730 --> 01:18:29.230
Yeah.

01:18:29.230 --> 01:18:32.990
Why did they keep the
human-version one [INAUDIBLE]?

01:18:36.070 --> 01:18:39.030
The full picture here
is that, at the time,

01:18:39.030 --> 01:18:43.670
the worm started learning
through behavioral cloning,

01:18:43.670 --> 01:18:45.853
and then they
basically collected

01:18:45.853 --> 01:18:47.270
data sets of expert
demonstrations

01:18:47.270 --> 01:18:51.310
and then they fine tuned this
through reinforcement learning.

01:18:51.310 --> 01:18:54.168
Jump starting this
work, they actually

01:18:54.168 --> 01:18:55.710
showed that the
behavior cloning part

01:18:55.710 --> 01:18:58.430
was detrimental to
final performance,

01:18:58.430 --> 01:19:00.970
and they just learned
everything through self-play.

01:19:00.970 --> 01:19:01.590
Yeah.

01:19:01.590 --> 01:19:02.210
Sorry.

01:19:02.210 --> 01:19:05.470
I know that [INAUDIBLE].

01:19:05.470 --> 01:19:08.970
You mentioned that like
every 500 iterations,

01:19:08.970 --> 01:19:12.370
if we updated one that it
was training itself off of,

01:19:12.370 --> 01:19:14.447
but the parameters have
found worked the best.

01:19:14.447 --> 01:19:16.530
Do you know if there's a
way that they avoided it,

01:19:16.530 --> 01:19:19.000
like converging on
some policy just

01:19:19.000 --> 01:19:22.220
one of those parameters that
the other one was playing with,

01:19:22.220 --> 01:19:24.175
if that makes sense?

01:19:24.175 --> 01:19:24.800
I don't think--

01:19:24.800 --> 01:19:26.480
Or was that part
of my [INAUDIBLE]?

01:19:26.480 --> 01:19:28.200
[INAUDIBLE] they were doing.

01:19:28.200 --> 01:19:32.880
I mean, the intuition there is
that you want increasing level

01:19:32.880 --> 01:19:36.820
of proficiency also for
your kind of adversary,

01:19:36.820 --> 01:19:39.857
if that makes sense.

01:19:39.857 --> 01:19:41.440
I'll have to double
check, but I think

01:19:41.440 --> 01:19:45.640
that just by the fact that you
are updating your adversary

01:19:45.640 --> 01:19:49.680
and you have this learning loop
that goes around this update,

01:19:49.680 --> 01:19:53.240
you'll always be incentivized
to find strategies that

01:19:53.240 --> 01:19:56.060
are better with respect
to your previous version,

01:19:56.060 --> 01:19:59.040
and so you'll improve hill
climbing in that sense.

01:19:59.040 --> 01:20:02.960
But maybe they did something
slightly more sophisticated.

01:20:02.960 --> 01:20:06.680
All right, so finish this
lecture on Wednesday.

01:20:06.680 --> 01:20:09.120
We'll also start
discussing model based

01:20:09.120 --> 01:20:10.920
reinforcement learning methods.

01:20:10.920 --> 01:20:12.710
Thank you.

